WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.689 --> 00:00:02.580
In the 21 years since author J.K.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.860
Rowling published the first entry into what
would become the wildly successful Harry Potter

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.140
book series, fans have concocted some pretty
far-out theories.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.060
There was the infamous one from 2004 that
argued the freckle-faced Ron Weasley was actually

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.240
Albus Dumbledore traveling back in time.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.790
There was the suggestion that Professor Minerva
McGonagall wasn't the trustworthy, kind, and

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.660
wise Head of Gryffindor everyone knew her
as, but was actually a Death Eater.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.120
There was even a Reddit fan theory that claimed
Ginny Weasley made love potions in the sixth

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.969
book, Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince,
and used them to induce Harry's feelings of

00:00:33.969 --> 00:00:35.680
intense infatuation for her.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.620
While Rowling hasn't been shy about addressing
fan theories in the past, there's one she

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.460
hasn't touched on until now – and it's one
Harry Potter fans have long suspected to be

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:43.960
true.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.240
These days, everybody knows how to pronounce
the name of Hogwarts' feistiest little wizard.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.380
Hermione
Hermione

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.219
Hermione
Harry! Hermione

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.420
Ms. Granger

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.760
But before 2000, it wasn't exactly common
knowledge.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.040
On Twitter, one user theorized that Rowling
included the passage about how to properly

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.230
pronounce Hermione Granger's name in the fourth
book, Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.120
"just to school all of us who were saying
HER-MY-OWN like Viktor Krum."

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.980
After Viktor repeatedly refers to her as "Hermy-own,"

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:12.480
 

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.600
"You're saying it wrong"

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.140
Hermione clarifies that her name is actually

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.180
pronounced "Her-my-oh-nee."

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.300
I shouldn't have said that I shouldn't have
said that."

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.490
When Harry Potter fans first read The Goblet
of Fire back when it was originally published

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.450
in 2000, they probably took the scene as little
more than a slightly awkward, but mostly chuckle-worthy

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.780
moment between the incredibly intelligent
Hermione and Viktor.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.360
As time went on, however, more and more readers
started to believe that the conversation had

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.780
a double meaning and served to subtly inform
fans about how to say Hermione's name the

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:45.480
correct way.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.740
Once Rowling caught wind of the theory, she
simply responded, "Theory correct."

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.040
"I can't believe it, she's done it again."

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.240
Sure, the theory isn't as bizarre as the ones
we mentioned before, but it's long been a

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.860
popular one amongst Potterheads.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.760
And it's a good thing that Rowling tossed
the small moment into The Goblet of Fire,

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.030
since it appears that countless Harry Potter
fans were saying Hermione's name all wrong

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.000
until she gave Viktor a pronunciation lesson.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.880
The Twitter account for the fan site Wizarding
News posted after Rowling confirmed the theory:

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.380
"I'm embarrassed it took three and a half
books to learn that pronunciation.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.220
Like many others, I had never seen the name
Hermione before in my life.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.840
Definitely pronounced it HER-ME-OWN in my
head for far too long."

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.720
"Honestly, don't you two read?"

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.920
Another Twitter user shared that she didn't
even know Hermione's name was Hermione because

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.780
her parents couldn't pronounce the character's
name when reading her the book at bedtime.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.040
"The most prominent memory I have of my parents
reading Harry Potter to me before bed is my

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.840
dad going 'Well that's a stupid name, from
now own I'm going to call her…

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.420
Jane.'"

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.680
"Soon we must all face a choice between what
is right and what is easy."

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:54.120
Thankfully, a year after Viktor got all tongue-tied
in The Goblet of Fire, which was published

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.900
in 2000, fans got to hear Emma Watson say
Hermione Granger's name in the first Harry

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.420
Potter movie.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.600
Ever since, there haven't been many mispronunciations.

